  <h2>Working Life</h2>

  <p>
    I spent roughly a decade on the data and software side of the private
    sector. I started at a then-<a href="https://www.novachem.com">Fortune 500 chemical
    company</a>, where I worked with the financial
    analytics team to design and maintain large SQL databases and built the
    reporting and Power BI dashboards that ran on top of them — extracting data
    from enterprise systems, reconciling it, and turning it into reports for
    audits and management. I also supported developers and built the
    documentation repositories that kept it all maintainable.
  </p>

  <p>
    From there I moved to smaller and mid-sized firms, eventually co-founding my
    own company. After selling it, I spent several years consulting on data
    analysis and reporting for businesses in the US and Southeast Asia before
    the COVID-19 pandemic. I then returned to graduate school to pursue
    economics and Southeast Asian studies — bringing the data-engineering
    background with me into research.
  </p>

  <h2>Education</h2>

  <p>
    I hold an MSc in Development Economics and an MA in Southeast Asian Studies from
    <a href="http://www.city.ac.uk">City, University of London</a> and
    <a href="https://www.soas.ac.uk/">SOAS, University of London</a> respectively.
    I did my undergraduate work at the <a href="http://www.pitt.edu">University of Pittsburgh</a>
    in Finance and Computer Science.
  </p>

  <h2>Currently</h2>

  <p>
    Writing a project on Viet Nam and US tariffs. Reading <em>Seeing Like a State</em>
    by James C. Scott.
  </p>
